# Vercel previews — don't spam builds

This project is limited to **100 Vercel deployments per day** on the account.
Every pushed branch that builds can consume one. Treat previews as a scarce budget,
not a default debugging step.

## Agent cap: 25 previews without asking

Do **not** push a branch or open a PR that will trigger a Vercel preview if doing
so would exceed **25 preview deployments in the current UTC day** without explicit
user authorization. When in doubt, or when the day’s preview count is unknown, ask
before pushing.

Reserve the remaining daily budget (~75) for production and user-authorized work.

`scripts/vercel-ignore.sh` already skips previews on `cursor/*` and `worktree-*`
branches, post-merge version bumps, and non-app diffs. Do not rely on that to spam
pushes — every forced build still counts against the cap.

## Do not push unless there is code to land

- No branch or PR for read-only investigation, Q&A, or tasks with nothing to merge.
- No WIP pushes just to "see it on Vercel" when `npm run build -w @party-tracker/app`
  and local Playwright/manual checks suffice.

## When a preview IS warranted

Push/open a PR only when deployed behavior is genuinely required — PWA/service
worker, Vercel routing or serverless runtime, production env vars, CDN headers, or
a defect that does not reproduce on local `npm run build && npm start`. Say in the
PR why a preview is needed.

## Which commits build on Vercel

**Do not restate this in agent docs** — `scripts/vercel-ignore.sh` (wired in
`vercel.json`) decides whether a commit needs a Vercel build. Read that script if
you need the path list. Avoid unnecessary branches anyway; the ignore step is not
permission to spam pushes.
